# iOS Port Prototype

The in-tree wrapper for Gecko on iOS is the minimal "GeckoTestBrowser"
application. When configure finds a Swift compiler (on macOS, the one from
Xcode), `./mach build` builds XUL, the GeckoView Swift wrapper, the app and its
BrowserEngineKit extensions, and assembles them into
`$objdir/dist/Applications/GeckoTestBrowser.app`. Without a Swift compiler,
only the core libraries such as XUL are built, and the Xcode project at
`mobile/ios/GeckoTestBrowser/GeckoTestBrowser.xcodeproj` bundles them into the
app instead.

## Simulator

### Building

1. Update your .mozconfig as described below:

```
# Enable the iOS application target.
ac_add_options --enable-application=mobile/ios
```

2. Add the Rust target for the simulator with
   `rustup target add aarch64-apple-ios-sim`.

3. Build with `./mach build` as normal.

### Running

Boot a simulator, then install and launch the app:

```
./mach run --url https://example.com/
```

`--url` sets the page the app opens at launch.

### Testing

Some mochitest-plain and xpcshell tests run on a booted simulator:

```
./mach mochitest testing/mochitest/tests/Harness_sanity/test_sanity.html
./mach xpcshell-test xpcom/tests/unit
```

The test harness needs a host `xpcshell`. Point `MOZ_HOST_BIN` at the
`dist/bin` directory of a macOS build, or let mach download host utilities
when it asks.
